From 9715e0dc6cd3594da8b20c3e6c25f6a6453457c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 10:05:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Say which stop the truck is actually driving to

A booking that collects at 9:14 PM and delivers at 3:14 AM opened the
customer's tracking page on "Scheduled arrival by 3:14 AM", under the
line "Next stop: Batangas Beverage Main Plant" - with the truck not yet
at the warehouse, and a collection between it and that branch.

Both halves of that were wrong in the same way. 3:14 AM is a true thing
about the delivery and a false thing about the next stop, and "Next stop"
is a claim about the truck, not about the customer. The page knew only
about BranchStops, so the first stop it could find was always the
customer's own, however many collections came first.

It read as a smaller mistake than it was while deliveries were booked
inside one working day, because the collection and the drop were a few
hours apart and both in the same morning. An overnight run - which the
booking form only started accepting last change - puts six hours and a
midnight between them, and the page announced the far end of that as the
next thing that would happen.

So the collection is on the page now. Which warehouse, when it is due,
and whether the crew have got there: nothing else about it, no address,
no contact, no quantity. The route drawn on the map already runs through
it, so naming it gives away nothing the map did not.

The delivery time is not given up for it. It is the one thing the
customer came here for, and it is carried alongside the collection time
rather than replaced by it, so the top of the page can say both - a
collection by 9:14 PM, a delivery expected by 3:14 AM - without either
being read as the other. The timeline gains the step it was missing
between "On the road" and the drop, and the drop stops being marked as
the stop in progress while the order is still in the warehouse.

Several collections are reported as one step. The customer is not
waiting at any of them, and which of three warehouses the crew are at
changes nothing they can do; that the order has not been picked up yet
does.

